WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.847 line:10%
Here's a puzzle. This muscle has no troponin.

2
00:00:02.847 --> 00:00:05.033 line:10%
So what does calcium switch on?

3
00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:08.333 line:10%
Calcium enters the cell through L-type channels —

4
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:11.633 line:10%
the dihydropyridine receptors that amlodipine will later block.

5
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.606 line:10%
There's a second route. IP₃ opens the store,

6
00:00:14.606 --> 00:00:17.800 line:10%
and the sarcoplasmic reticulum adds calcium of its own.

7
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.072 line:10%
Calcium binds calmodulin.

8
00:00:19.072 --> 00:00:22.725 line:10%
That complex switches on myosin light chain kinase,

9
00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:25.433 line:10%
which puts a phosphate on the myosin.

10
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:26.428 line:10%
Now the rule.

11
00:00:26.428 --> 00:00:29.962 line:10%
Only a phosphorylated head can bind actin and pull.

12
00:00:29.962 --> 00:00:32.500 line:10%
The unphosphorylated ones sit there, useless.

13
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.878 line:10%
Take the phosphate off,

14
00:00:33.878 --> 00:00:36.633 line:10%
and the head doesn't let go. That's latch.

15
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:39.289 line:10%
Myosin light chain phosphatase removes the phosphate.

16
00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:41.260 line:10%
It never stopped working —

17
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.600 line:10%
tone is simply the balance between the two enzymes.

18
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.504 line:10%
Here's resting tone: the kinase winning slightly.

19
00:00:47.504 --> 00:00:51.254 line:10%
Now cAMP blocks it. No new phosphate goes on,

20
00:00:51.254 --> 00:00:53.767 line:10%
and the phosphatase wins by default.

21
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:57.198 line:10%
The endothelium does it the other way.

22
00:00:57.198 --> 00:01:01.119 line:10%
Nitric oxide diffuses across, switches on guanylyl cyclase,

23
00:01:01.119 --> 00:01:05.162 line:10%
and the cGMP it makes activates the phosphatase.

24
00:01:05.162 --> 00:01:07.000 line:10%
Same endpoint, opposite enzyme.

25
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.425 line:10%
Acetylcholine dilates — but only by asking

26
00:01:09.425 --> 00:01:11.694 line:10%
the endothelium to make the nitric oxide.

27
00:01:11.694 --> 00:01:14.119 line:10%
Damage that cell, and the same acetylcholine

28
00:01:14.119 --> 00:01:16.700 line:10%
does nothing at all. Watch it fail.

29
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.229 line:10%
Nitroprusside doesn't ask. It carries nitric oxide

30
00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:22.014 line:10%
in and releases it inside the muscle cell.

31
00:01:22.014 --> 00:01:23.819 line:10%
The endothelium is still dead,

32
00:01:23.819 --> 00:01:25.733 line:10%
and the vessel opens anyway.

33
00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:26.769 line:10%
Amlodipine —

34
00:01:26.769 --> 00:01:29.770 line:10%
a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker —

35
00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.079 line:10%
plugs the L-type channel. Calcium can't get in.

36
00:01:33.079 --> 00:01:35.908 line:10%
The pool falls, the kinase goes quiet,

37
00:01:35.908 --> 00:01:39.141 line:10%
the phosphates come off, and the vessel opens.

38
00:01:39.141 --> 00:01:42.483 line:10%
The pool falls because the pumps never stopped —

39
00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:44.907 line:10%
SERCA, PMCA and the sodium–calcium exchanger.

40
00:01:44.907 --> 00:01:46.928 line:10%
Block entry and removal wins,

41
00:01:46.928 --> 00:01:48.767 line:10%
exactly like the phosphatase did.

42
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:50.477 line:10%
Tone is a tug of war,

43
00:01:50.477 --> 00:01:52.517 line:10%
and every one of these drugs

44
00:01:52.517 --> 00:01:53.657 line:10%
just changes who wins.

45
00:01:53.657 --> 00:01:55.652 line:10%
Amlodipine dilates arterioles but not veins —

46
00:01:55.652 --> 00:01:57.033 line:10%
which is why the ankles swell.

47
00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:59.178 line:10%
So: no troponin. Calcium binds calmodulin,

48
00:01:59.178 --> 00:02:01.680 line:10%
and the switch sits on the myosin.

49
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.824 line:10%
The kinase puts a phosphate on;

50
00:02:03.824 --> 00:02:07.383 line:10%
the phosphatase takes it off; tone is the balance.

51
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:08.813 line:10%
A latched head holds

52
00:02:08.813 --> 00:02:11.096 line:10%
that tone for hours on almost nothing.

53
00:02:11.096 --> 00:02:13.795 line:10%
Sustained tone needs calcium from outside —

54
00:02:13.795 --> 00:02:16.067 line:10%
which is the door amlodipine closes.

55
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:18.570 line:10%
And it dilates arterioles but not veins,

56
00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.267 line:10%
which is why the ankles swell.
